sections.s2_technical:
Interface projection supports SpEL `@Value` for computed fields — something DTO projections cannot do:

```java
// Interface with computed field via SpEL:
public interface AccountSummary {
    String getAccountId();
    String getHolderName();
    BigDecimal getBalance();

    @Value("#{target.holderName + ' (' + target.accountId + ')'}")
    String getDisplayName();  // computed at runtime: "Rajesh Kumar (SAV2001)"
}
// Spring generates proxy: getDisplayName() evaluates SpEL against the target entity.
// SQL still only fetches: account_id, holder_name, balance.
```

DTO projection with nested projections for associations:

```java
// DTO for account with trade summary:
public record AccountWithTrades(
    String accountId,
    String holderName,
    BigDecimal balance,
    List<TradeSummary> trades  // nested projection!
) {}

public record TradeSummary(String tradeId, String symbol, BigDecimal amount) {}

// JPQL constructor expression doesn't support nested collections.
// Use interface projection with nested interface for this:
public interface AccountWithTradesView {
    String getAccountId();
    String getHolderName();
    List<TradeView> getTrades();  // nested interface projection!

    interface TradeView {
        String getTradeId();
        String getSymbol();
    }
}
```

Why projections beat full entity — concrete numbers:

```java
// Entity: 20 columns, 500 bytes per row, 10K rows
// Full entity: 20 columns × 10K = 200K field reads + 10K snapshots (dirty checking)
//   Memory: ~5MB data + ~5MB snapshots = 10MB
//   Network: 500 bytes × 10K = 5MB from DB

// DTO (3 columns): 3 columns × 10K = 30K field reads, ZERO snapshots
//   Memory: ~300KB (no persistence context!)
//   Network: 75 bytes × 10K = 750KB from DB
//   Savings: 85% less data, 94% less memory, zero dirty checking
```

